data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc` into a `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** Both work as expected:

```csharp
Span<nint>  signed   = stackalloc nint[4];
Span<nuint> unsigned = stackalloc nuint[4];
```

On this 64-bit machine, `sizeof(nint)` is 8 bytes, so each element is pointer-sized. The feature requires `unsafe` context (or you can use it in a safe context if you assign directly to a `Span<T>` — but `nint`/`nuint` stackalloc still needs `AllowUnsafeBlocks` enabled).
